    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Corbin Carroll** (76.20) and **James Wood** (73.06) anchoring the outfield as our clear-cut priority starts. **Corbin Carroll** thrives against Michael King, leveraging an 11% SP skill boost despite the moderate hold-runner risk. Meanwhile, **James Wood** is our premier play in the leadoff spot; his elite .429 xwOBA projection combined with a favorable matchup against Arrighetti makes him the cornerstone of our lineup. **Josh Naylor** (72.36) also remains a mandatory start against Phillips, buoyed by a significant 19% SP skill advantage that keeps his ceiling high despite a slight park penalty.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved selecting the final utility spot, where **Kyle Karros** (54.01) narrowly edged out **Cole Young** (51.08). While **Cole Young** benefits from the same favorable matchup against Phillips, the optimizer leaned into the 8% order boost for **Kyle Karros** hitting second, even against the daunting Roki Sasaki. We are prioritizing the speed-scoring potential of **Mickey Moniak** (63.93) today as well; despite the lineup being pending, his 14% platoon edge against a right-handed starter and his ability to exploit the slow delivery of Roki Sasaki make him a high-efficiency play for stolen bases.\n\nWe must remain cautious with **Seiya Suzuki** (51.88) and **Austin Riley** (53.79), as both are hovering near our \"marginal\" threshold. **Seiya Suzuki** faces the stiffest test of the day against Kremer, whose 2.85 xERA heavily suppresses the projected scoring potential despite a generous park factor. Additionally, we are monitoring the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Dalton Rushing**, **Alex Freeland**, **Mickey Moniak**, and **Kyle Karros**. If any of these players drop significantly in the order, their efficiency scores\u2014particularly those of **Alex Freeland** and **Dalton Rushing**\u2014could dip below the 50-point floor, forcing a late-game pivot to bench depth like **Isaac Paredes**.",
